  "articleBody": "Installing a ramp seems straightforward, but there are a few common slip-ups people make. Let's make sure you don't fall into these traps! \n\n1.  **Measuring Only One Side of the Threshold:** It's super important to measure the height across the entire threshold, not just one spot. Uneven floors happen, and you need that full picture for an accurate 'rise' measurement.\n\n2.  **Ignoring Door-Swing Interference:** Always check how your door opens and closes with the ramp in place. A ramp that blocks your door is more of a headache than a help. Make sure there’s enough clearance for smooth operation.\n\n3.  **Choosing a Ramp That's Too Steep:** The golden rule for accessibility is often a 1:12 slope – that means for every 1 inch of rise, you need 12 inches of ramp length. A steeper ramp can be really difficult or even unsafe for mobility devices.\n\n4.  **Failing to Confirm Weight Capacity:** Ramps need to support their users. Always double-check the manufacturer's weight capacity to ensure it's robust enough for the intended use.\n\n5.  **Overlooking Surface Conditions:** Is the ground underneath stable? Will the ramp shift? Make sure the surface is solid and that the ramp can be securely installed per manufacturer instructions. If you’re facing a complex situation, or need to ensure compliance, getting a professional involved can prevent these mistakes and ensure a safe, lasting solution.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
